+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit can appear at most once, in that order, with no spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive, so `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, and `1d2h3m4s` are all accepted. Negative numbers, decimals, and unknown,
+repeated, or out-of-order units are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` default is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
